        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "Yes \u2014 seaweed does have fiber.  \n\nSeaweed (like nori, wakame, kelp, and kombu) is naturally rich in **dietary fiber**, particularly **soluble fiber** such as alginates, carrageenan, and agar. These fibers can help with digestion, promote a feeling of fullness, and support gut health.  \n\n- **Nori (the sheets used for sushi)** has about **0.5\u20131 g of fiber per sheet**.  \n- **Wakame and kelp** (used in soups or salads) are also good fiber sources, usually providing **2\u20134 g per 100 g (cooked)**.  \n- Seaweed snacks (roasted nori chips) are usually low in calories but still give you a small fiber boost.  \n\nWould you like me to pull together a quick list of **lowest-fiber sushi options** for you, since seaweed is usually included in sushi?"
          ]
